# Sri Lanka Declares State of Emergency as Cyclone Ditwah Claims 132 Lives

Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday, appealing for international aid as Cyclone Ditwah's devastating rains and floods claimed 132 lives and left 176 individuals unaccounted for.

According to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), as reported by AFP, the severe weather system obliterated over 15,000 homes and compelled 78,000 people to seek refuge in government-run temporary shelters. "We have 132 confirmed dead and another 176 missing," confirmed DMC Director-General Sampath Kotuwegoda, who further mentioned that relief efforts have been bolstered with deployments from the army, navy, and air force.

As the cyclone tracked north toward India on Saturday, new landslides occurred in Kandy, 115 km (70 miles) east of Colombo, inundating several sections of the primary access road. Concurrently, worsening floods in low-lying regions led authorities to mandate evacuations for residents along the Kelani River, which empties into the Indian Ocean from Colombo.

The government appealed for international aid and called on overseas Sri Lankans to donate funds to support nearly half a million people impacted by the calamity.

India promptly responded to the crisis, dispatching two aircraft loaded with relief items. An Indian warship docked in Colombo for a goodwill visit contributed its provisions to support victims. Prime Minister Narendra Modi, via a post on X, conveyed his condolences to the Sri Lankan populace, mourning the lives lost due to the cyclone and reaffirming India's unwavering support for its "closest maritime neighbour." He emphasized that India had swiftly dispatched relief supplies and essential Humanitarian Assistance and Disaster Relief (HADR) equipment as part of the response efforts.
