Deputy Minister Phung Duc Tien remarked that the nation might still face its 16th storm of the year as 2025 draws to a close.

Hoang Duc Cuong, Deputy Director of the Hydrometeorological Department, presented an overview of the year's natural disaster and hydro-meteorological developments, highlighting that 2025 has experienced highly atypical weather patterns and disasters, resulting in significant damage across the country.

Notably, the Bach Ma station in Thua Thien Hue recorded an extraordinary 1,739 mm of rainfall within a single day in October. The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is in the process of evaluating this figure for possible acknowledgment as one of the greatest 24-hour rainfall totals ever documented worldwide.

In the East Sea, the frequency of storms and tropical depressions has hit its peak since Vietnam initiated record-keeping in 1961. By the close of November, a total of 21 storms and tropical depressions had been documented, exceeding the former high of 20 established in 2017.

The 2025 storm season was marked by a host of uncommon and severe events. Storm No. 1 (WUTIP) emerged in the East Sea in June, an occurrence not witnessed in over four decades. Storm No. 9 (RAGASA) achieved level 17 wind speeds, with gusts surpassing this level, marking it as the most powerful storm ever documented in the East Sea. During late November, a tropical depression transitioned from the Indian Ocean to the northwestern Pacific, marking an unprecedented path in the annals of meteorological records.

The floods of 2025 were notably extensive and intense. Many rivers experienced peak water levels that exceeded past historical highs. For the first time in a single year, 20 rivers recorded either extreme or historic flooding, encompassing the Cau River (Thai Nguyen, Bac Ninh), the Thuong River (Bac Giang), the Bang Giang River (Cao Bang), and the Lo River (Tuyen Quang). These floods stretched from the Northern and Central territories to the Central Highlands, further impacting the lower Mekong Delta. Significantly, the historic flood in eastern Dak Lak, formerly part of Phu Yen province, surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin.

Deputy Director Hoang Duc Cuong observed that the array of atypical occurrences in Vietnam and globally during 2025 indicates a shift in conventional flood patterns. The pattern of intensifying weather extremes is becoming more evident, with notable variations in storms, precipitation, and flooding directly impacted by climate change.
