Thousands of residents in Vietnam were compelled to evacuate their homes following unprecedented rainfall that inundated central provinces since the weekend. The environment ministry reported on Tuesday that over 8,600 individuals across four central provinces have sought refuge in schools and other public facilities, prompted by threats of severe flooding and landslides.

The central coastal region has been deluged with heavy rain since Saturday, with rainfall exceeding one meter within a single day in some areas. The historic city of Hue, recognized as a UNESCO world heritage site, was submerged by the flooding. Educational institutions in Hue and Danang were shut down starting Saturday, and flooding caused disruptions on the railway connecting the northern and southern parts of the country.

In Hoi An, tourists were captured by state media maneuvering boats through the town's narrow lanes, while journalists reported on evacuation efforts in severely inundated areas.

Experts attribute the increased lethality and destruction of extreme weather phenomena, such as storms and floods, to climate change driven by human activities. In early October, Vietnam's rugged northern regions and its capital, Hanoi, experienced severe flooding due to typhoons Bualoi and Matmo.
