Global financial losses due to natural disasters significantly decreased to $224 billion in 2025, yet Munich Re, a German reinsurance firm, cautioned on January 13 about a persistently "alarming" outlook on extreme weather events, with climate change likely being a significant contributing factor.

The figure decreased by almost 40% compared to the previous year, partly due to the fact that, for the first time in several years, no hurricanes made landfall on the US mainland.

Nevertheless, "the overarching outlook for 2025 raised significant concerns about floods, intense storms, and wildfires," Munich Re stated in its annual disaster report.

According to the group, by mere happenstance, the globe narrowly avoided suffering even greater losses.

"The Earth is experiencing a fever, leading to a surge in extreme and severe weather occurrences," stated Tobias Grimm, chief climate scientist at Munich Re.

The Los Angeles wildfires in January emerged as the year's most expensive catastrophe, resulting in total damages of $53 billion and approximately $40 billion in insured losses.

Insured losses for 2025 totaled $108 billion, marking a significant decrease from the previous year.

Approximately 17,200 individuals perished due to natural disasters globally, a notable increase from the roughly 11,000 recorded in 2024, though falling short of the 10-year average tally of 17,800.

Grimm described 2025 as a year possessing "two faces." "The insurance industry faced unprecedented losses during the first half of the year," he remarked, "yet the latter half recorded the minimal losses of the past ten years."

Currently, it is the collective expenses from smaller-scale calamities, such as regional floods and wildfires, that are exerting the most significant impact. Munich Re reported that these incidents led to losses totaling $166 billion last year.

Following the LA wildfires, the most expensive catastrophe of the year was a catastrophic earthquake that struck Myanmar in March, causing an estimated $12 billion in damages, with only a minimal portion being covered by insurance.

The financial impact of tropical cyclones amounted to approximately $37 billion in damages. Hurricane Melissa, among the most powerful storms to hit land, ravaged Jamaica, resulting in approximately $9.8 billion in damages.

Regionally, the United States faced total losses of $118 billion, with $88 billion covered by insurance, closely aligning with a $115 billion total loss estimate provided by the US nonprofit Climate Central.

The Asia-Pacific region experienced losses amounting to approximately $73 billion, yet just $9 billion of those losses were covered by insurance. Australia experienced its second-highest financial impact from natural disasters since 1980, primarily driven by a succession of intense storms and widespread flooding.

Europe incurred losses amounting to $11 billion. In Africa, natural disasters resulted in $3 billion in damages, with insurance covering less than 20% of that total.

The report emerges amid growing doubts about green policies, especially following the return to power of US President Donald Trump, who dismisses climate science as a "hoax."

"Increased heat results in heightened humidity, intensified rainfall, and higher wind speeds," Grimm remarked, noting that climate change is already playing a role in intensifying extreme weather.
