The Labor government is set to allocate $200 million towards 96 projects across the nation, aimed at bolstering community resilience against severe weather conditions.

From the total $200 million allocated by the federal government, over half will be allocated to infrastructure projects with immediate construction initiation.

Approved projects will enable various natural disaster relief initiatives, including establishing recovery centres, flood protection infrastructure, reducing the dangers posed by bushfires, and implementing sophisticated alert systems for natural threats.

This includes initiatives on Palm Island, a coastal area off Queensland known for its increased vulnerability to tropical cyclones and severe weather events, where a cyclone shelter and evacuation centre capable of accommodating 800 people will be constructed.

Additional initiatives feature a $14.1 million investment for enhancing stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera, located in Central NSW; a $1.6 million infrastructure undertaking aimed at safeguarding Townsvale Estate in South Australia; and $895k to enhance the capacity of Karajarri Rangers, focusing on reducing severe wildfire threats in northwest WA.

This declaration marks the third round of financial support from the $1bn Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), a governmental program dedicated to enhancing disaster resilience and mitigating risks.

In the past two rounds, the DRF has distributed approximately $400 million in federal funding to over 350 projects.

Almost fifty percent of these projects are currently in the phases of pre-construction or construction-delivery, with 43 projects either approaching completion or fully finished, as reported by the department.

"Australians are well-acquainted with the annual onslaught of floods, bushfires, and cyclones, which is precisely why the Albanese government initiated the Disaster Ready Fund, allocating up to $1 billion over the span of five years," stated Kristy McBain, the emergency management minister.

The third round will advantage every one of the 538 local government areas spanning all states and territories.

"From constructing cyclone shelters on Palm Island to strategizing flood mitigation in Launceston," McBain noted.

"Importantly, the third round elevates our investment to make Australia more resilient through the DRF to $600 million – and I am grateful to numerous project backers for matching our funding," she added.
