In the first nine months of 2025, extreme weather events in India were linked to as many as 4,064 fatalities, according to a joint report from the Delhi-based think tank Centre for Science and Environment (CSE) and Down To Earth (DTE). The analysis indicated this figure represented a 48 percent rise over the past four years.

Madhya Pradesh recorded the highest number of fatalities. Maharashtra experienced the most significant impact in terms of affected cropped area, totaling 8.4 million hectares.

The impact of these climatic events—including heat and cold waves, lightning, storms, heavy rainfall, floods, and landslides—resulted in crop damage reaching an astonishing 9.47 million hectares, marking a 400 percent increase over four years.

Throughout this period, the nation experienced some form of extreme weather conditions on 99 percent of the days, resulting in these recorded losses. A minimum of 18 states or Union territories documented their peak count of extreme weather days since 2022, as indicated by the report.

From February through September in 2025, no fewer than 30 states and Union Territories faced persistent extreme weather conditions for eight consecutive months. Himachal Pradesh witnessed the highest number of these events.

Among the 4,064 fatalities recorded in 2025, 3,007 occurred during the monsoon season. India's monsoon continues to be the most destructive season.

Released annually, the Climate India 2025 report draws upon close to 1,500 days of continuous observation, documenting seasonal patterns in extreme weather beginning in 2022. The report was unveiled on November 19, 2025, during a virtual seminar hosted by Sunita Narain, the director general of CSE and editor for DTE.

"Given the intensity and frequency of extreme weather events, the country no longer needs to count just the disasters. What we need to understand is the scale—the scale of mitigation that Belém is talking about, the scale on which the whole world has to come together. But it is also about what we need to do, keeping in mind that there will be more and more such disasters," she remarked.
