# Sri Lankan Officials Battle Escalating Floodwaters in Capital After Deadly Cyclone

COLOMBO: On Sunday, Sri Lankan officials contended with escalating floodwaters affecting sections of the capital following the devastation wrought by a formidable cyclone.

Colombo's northern region was contending with severe flooding, while the Kelani River's water level kept climbing, according to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC). Dozens of families remain stranded in their homes due to rising floodwaters in Colombo.

The number of fatalities has risen to 159 after a week of intense rainfall caused by Cyclone Ditwah, which departed from Sri Lanka on Saturday. An additional 203 individuals remain unaccounted for.

"Intense rainfall in the upstream regions is currently causing flooding in the low-lying areas adjacent to the Kelani River banks," stated a DMC official. "Even though the cyclone has passed, significant rainfall upstream is causing flooding in these low-lying regions."

On Saturday, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake announced a state of emergency to address the cyclone's aftermath and sought assistance from the international community.

According to an official, close to 200,000 individuals have been saved and relocated to shelters. Over 20,000 homes lie in ruins due to the severe weather system, displacing 122,000 individuals who now reside in government-operated temporary shelters. An additional 833,000 individuals needed aid after being uprooted by the flooding.

Personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been dispatched to work in conjunction with civilian workers and volunteers, contributing to the relief efforts.

India promptly took the lead by dispatching relief supplies along with two helicopters and their respective crews for rescue operations. Japan announced plans to dispatch a team to evaluate urgent requirements and committed to providing additional support.

