The Syrian National Earthquake Center participated in an advanced workshop held in Algeria on November 11-12, centered on evaluating earthquake hazards and instructing attendees on sophisticated systems.

The Arab Center for Earthquake and Natural Disaster Prevention orchestrated the event, which partnered with the Global Earthquake Model (GEM) Foundation and successfully convened specialists from ten Arab nations.

The workshop educated participants in using the OpenQuake Engine software, guiding them in crafting detailed seismic hazard models and generating risk maps to evaluate potential earthquake impacts.

Jamal Zakkour, who leads the National Earthquake Center, remarked that the workshop offered significant insights into how Algeria assesses the risk of earthquakes. He noted that it facilitated the sharing of expertise and contemporary methods among the involved nations, enhancing comprehension of seismic phenomena in the area and bolstering disaster response plans.

Zakkour highlighted that the training encompassed developing accurate models to evaluate earthquake hazards and their possible impacts.

Syria's involvement is consistent with its initiatives to bolster scientific readiness as part of the Ministry of Emergency and Disaster Management's strategy.

Established in 2004, the Arab Center for Earthquake and Natural Disaster Prevention aims to enhance regional collaboration in the fields of earthquake science and the mitigation of natural disaster risks. From its inception, the center has focused on enhancing regional readiness by fostering advanced scientific education and collaborative efforts.
