A remarkably intense storm struck in November, churning over the South China Sea as it neared Vietnam. Its winds were consistently blowing at speeds of approximately 114 mph, with gusts potentially climbing to 137 mph.

Vietnam's central provinces are currently experiencing severe flooding due to record-breaking rains. Kalmaegi is predicted to deliver over 24 inches of rain in certain regions. In coastal cities like Danang, waves up to 10ft high battered the coast and robust winds have toppled trees in Dak Lak province. Numerous residences in Quy Nhon, another coastal city, experienced prolonged power outages. Ho Chi Minh City, the nation's economic center, confronts an increased threat of significant flooding. High tides were anticipated on the Saigon River, with officials cautioning that as much as 4 inches of rain might flood regions at lower elevations.

Spanning the central regions of the Philippines, Kalmaegi claimed the lives of at least 114 individuals and left 127 unaccounted for, marking the most devastating natural catastrophe to strike the nation this year. On Thursday, President Ferdinand Marcos Jr declared a state of emergency.

Having unleashed devastation across the Philippines, with the central province of Cebu bearing the brunt, the tropical cyclone exited the archipelago on Wednesday, heading into the South China Sea.

According to the Office of Civil Defence, the typhoon forced the displacement of over 560,000 villagers in the Philippines, with close to 450,000 seeking refuge in emergency shelters. With the declaration of a "state of national calamity," the government is enabled to expedite the release of emergency funds and curb both food hoarding and excessive pricing.

Kalmaegi unleashed a deluge equivalent to one-and-a-half months of precipitation in merely one day on Tuesday across metropolitan Cebu, according to state forecaster Benison Estareja. Heavy rains triggered flash floods, swelling a river along with other waterways in Cebu city and its neighboring towns. Torrential floodwaters engulfed or carried off numerous vehicles in Cebu's residential areas, with dramatic footage captured by residents who were marooned on rooftops.

A minimum of 71 fatalities occurred in Cebu, primarily attributed to drowning incidents. Authorities also noted that an additional 62 individuals were unaccounted for in the central province of Negros Occidental, close to Cebu.

In the town of Liloan, Cebu, Krizza Espra made her way to the mortuary on Thursday, where the bodies of her husband and three children, who perished when their roof caved in, were kept in preparation for a wake. She explained that four members of her family — her mother and aunt among them — are still unaccounted for. "I hope someone can help speed up the search," she said.

Six fatalities linked to Kalmaegi occurred when a Philippine air force helicopter crashed in the southern province of Agusan del Sur on Tuesday. According to the military, the crew was en route to deliver humanitarian aid to areas heavily impacted by the typhoon.

"We prepared as much as possible for the typhoon, but, you know, certain surprises like flash floods are truly beyond expectation," Governor Pamela Baricuatro told The Associated Press by telephone. Ms. Baricuatro stated that the storm's impacts might have been aggravated by quarrying activities that led to river blockage and overflow, combined with inadequate flood control measures in Cebu province.

A scandal involving corruption in inadequate or entirely absent flood control projects throughout the Philippines has ignited public indignation and led to street demonstrations in recent months.

Additionally, Cebu continues to recuperate from the aftermath of a 6.9 magnitude earthquake that struck on September 30, which resulted in at least 79 fatalities and the displacement of numerous individuals.

Officials in charge of disaster response have cautioned that an approaching tropical cyclone from the Pacific may intensify into a super typhoon, potentially striking the northern Philippines at the start of next week.
