India holds the 9th position among nations most severely impacted by climate-related catastrophes over the past three decades, according to the newest Climate Risk Index (CRI) report published by Germanwatch, a non-governmental organization headquartered in Bonn.

In the most recent preparation of this index, completed in 2023, India secured the eighth position.

India has also seen an improvement in its position on the list of countries most severely impacted by disasters last year, shifting from 10th place in 2023 to 15th currently.

A decline in the occurrence of disasters might be responsible for this shift in India, yet it could equally signify the nation's ongoing enhancement of its resilience.

The report was developed through an analysis of current and past climate data accessible in a public forum and was unveiled on the second day of the ongoing COP30 climate summit taking place in Belém, Brazil.
