The World Meteorological Organization, under the United Nations, advocates for the enhancement of worldwide monitoring and prediction systems to warn individuals in developing countries about severe weather conditions.

"Early warning means early action," asserted WMO Secretary-General Celeste Saulo during her address at the UN's yearly conference in Geneva. "Our objective is to not only warn the world; it is to empower it."

A shift in the global climate has led to more frequent and severe occurrences of extreme weather events. The UN's meteorological agency has noted that over the past five decades, climate-related incidents have resulted in the deaths of more than 2 million individuals.

Almost 50% of nations are particularly susceptible to these climatic phenomena, with developing countries facing heightened danger due to inadequate alert systems. The agency expresses its worry over insufficient safeguards against these weather occurrences and asserts that implementing early warning systems could avert most fatalities attributed to weather conditions.

Half of the countries utilizing the systems are engaging only with their fundamental features, while 16% are operating below even these basic levels. The WMO noted that while advancements have been achieved and more nations are adopting early warning systems, significant efforts are still needed.

A preemptive alert mechanism offers additional time for evacuation and readiness in anticipation of a severe weather incident impacting a region. Such occurrences encompass hurricanes, flooding, wildfires, and the disintegration of glaciers. Early warning systems are crucial for providing individuals with the chance to strengthen their surroundings or evacuate them safely.

Broader implementation of early warning systems has the potential to preserve lives. The WMO is striving to enhance recognition of the underuse of the early warning system for extreme weather occurrences. With the establishment of operational systems in additional countries, the preservation of lives will increase.

The WMO emphasized the rise in climate-related weather incidents, noting that "impacts are escalating as weather patterns grow more severe." The article highlighted that advancements are underway, yet further efforts are necessary.
