It is believed to be the largest instance of fly-tipping in the UK.

This enormous heap of waste spans 500 feet in length, resembling a 'river', and reaches a depth of 20 feet. The waste was illicitly discarded all at once in a field adjacent to the A34 and River Cherwell near Kidlington in Oxfordshire.

The massive pile was discovered last month by horrified anglers and comprises materials such as plastic, foam, and wood. An MP has characterized this act as threatening "an environmental disaster".

Friends of the Thames, the charity, informed the BBC: "This is an environmental catastrophe unfolding in plain sight. With each passing day, the likelihood of toxic run-off seeping into the river system grows, endangering wildlife and compromising the health of the whole catchment area."

Due to the considerable height of some of the waste, portions have collapsed, resulting in a distance of approximately five metres from the river. Heatmaps show that the waste is also heating up, increasing the likelihood of a fire.

The Environment Agency has verified that an investigation is underway. An Environment Agency spokesperson stated: "Specialist officers are currently probing the waste disposal incident near the A34 at Kidlington. Their task is to identify the party who left the waste there and take suitable measures."

"We share the public's anger about incidents like this, which is why we take action against those responsible for waste crime. If you have details about this incident or suspect waste crime elsewhere, contact the Environment Agency's 24-hour incident hotline: 0800 807060."

Calum Miller, representing Bicester and Woodstock for the Liberal Democrats, brought attention to the matter in Parliament on Thursday, stating: "Criminals have offloaded a massive pile of illegal plastic waste weighing several hundred tonnes, within my constituency on a floodplain next to the River Cherwell. Water levels in rivers are climbing."

The Environment Agency indicated that its enforcement capabilities are constrained by limited resources, noting that the projected expense of removal exceeds the total yearly budget of the local district council.
