# India Ranks Ninth Among Countries Most Impacted by Extreme Weather Events Over Three Decades

According to the latest Climate Risk Index (CRI) 2026 report by Germanwatch, India ranked ninth among the countries most impacted by extreme weather events from 1995 to 2024.

The evaluation was unveiled at the current COP30 event in Belem, Brazil. This marks the second edition of the report to be issued this year.

The CRI derives its ranking from the economic and human impacts caused by extreme weather conditions. The higher a country's position on the list, the more severely it has been impacted by extreme weather events linked to human-induced climate change. All top ten countries featured in the list are situated within the Global South.

The index examines rapid onset occurrences such as floods, storms, extreme temperatures, wildfires, glacial lake outbursts, and floods. It excludes slow onset phenomena like increasing mean temperatures, sea level rise, ocean acidification, and glacial retreat, among others.

In 2024, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines in the Caribbean suffered the most severe impact, with Grenada in the same area experiencing the second highest level of adversity, and Chad in central Africa also among the most affected. Regarding the impact of extreme weather in 2024, India placed third after Bangladesh and the Philippines, ranking at 15th place in the single-year rankings for 2024.

Over the past thirty years, India has experienced approximately 430 extreme weather events, including floods, landslides, heatwaves, cyclones, and droughts, impacting over a billion people nationwide. These events have caused inflation-adjusted damages totaling approximately $170 billion, and have claimed the lives of over 80,000 individuals.

The most devastating calamity in 2024 was flooding, impacting nearly 50 million individuals globally that year. Subsequently, heat waves impacted approximately 33 million individuals, while droughts influenced more than 29 million. One of the most catastrophic incidents was Typhoon Trami, which struck the Philippines in October, resulting in over a hundred fatalities and impacting millions of lives.

India faced severe devastation during the 2024 monsoon season, impacting over 8 million individuals predominantly in the regions of Gujarat, Maharashtra, and Tripura.

The report classified India, alongside the Philippines, Nicaragua, and Haiti, under the "continuous threats" category, indicating that these nations face persistent and recurrent extreme weather conditions. This repeated exposure is also the reason India ranks prominently in the long-term, 30-year analysis, as the cumulative impact of successive disasters leads to escalating losses over time.

Nations like Haiti, the Philippines, and India—each ranking among the ten most affected in the CRI—confront unique obstacles. They endure such frequent onslaughts of floods, heatwaves, or storms that whole areas scarcely have time to recuperate from one disaster before the next one arrives. During COP discussions, the spotlight on funding for loss and damage primarily targets these countries. Absent enhanced long-term backing, especially for climate crisis adaptation, they will confront overwhelming obstacles.

Dominica, Myanmar, Honduras, and Libya face elevated vulnerability due to their exposure to exceptionally severe weather conditions. In 2008, Cyclone Nargis struck Myanmar, marking one of the most devastating catastrophes in the past thirty years. It resulted in the deaths of approximately 140,000 individuals, comprising over 95% of the country's fatalities during this timeframe.

From 1995 to 2024, over 832,000 individuals worldwide lost their lives due to 9,700 extreme weather-related incidents. Additionally, these events inflicted economic damages amounting to more than $4.5 trillion.

The CRI analysis highlights that India is significantly impacted by frequent extreme weather phenomena, which have consistently influenced the populace and the economy over time. The report further recognizes that data deficiencies concerning the Global South might imply that the true frequency and consequences of extreme weather events are potentially more severe than current understanding.

"The CRI 2026 outcomes underscore the necessity for COP30 to identify practical strategies for bridging the global ambition shortfall. Immediate reduction of global emissions is crucial to prevent an increase in fatalities and global economic catastrophes. Simultaneously, it is imperative to speed up adaptation initiatives. Implementing effective strategies to address loss and damage is crucial, alongside ensuring sufficient climate finance is available," stated David Eckstein, senior advisor on climate finance and investments at Germanwatch.

As Vera Künzel, senior advisor on climate change adaptation and human rights at Germanwatch, noted, these nations will encounter overwhelming obstacles without enhanced long-term assistance, particularly in adapting to the climate crisis.
