Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday and appealed for international assistance as the fatalities caused by floods and mudslides due to Cyclone Ditwah climbed to 153, with an additional 191 individuals unaccounted for.

The severe weather conditions have led to the destruction of over 20,000 homes, forcing 108,000 individuals to seek refuge in government-operated temporary shelters. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) announced that an additional 798,000 individuals needed support following their temporary displacement due to the floods.

President Anura Kumara Dissanayake exercised emergency powers, providing him extensive control to address the destruction following a week of relentless rainfall that battered the island.

DMC spokesman Pradeep Kodippili verified that 153 individuals had lost their lives. Among them were 11 residents of an elder care home that was flooded in the north-central district of Kurunegala on Saturday afternoon, police said.

Military personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been mobilized in conjunction with civilian employees and volunteers to aid in the relief operations. On Saturday, the military successfully rescued 69 bus passengers, among them a German tourist, who had been stranded in the Anuradhapura district following a 24-hour operation.

While recovering in the hospital, a passenger recounted the assistance they received from navy sailors, who employed ropes to safely navigate them through the floodwaters and onto the roof of a nearby house. "We were very lucky," remarked W. M. Shantha. "As we stood on the roof, a section gave way and three women plunged into the water, yet they were assisted back to safety on the roof." A helicopter was forced to abandon the first rescue effort as the rotor's downdraft risked dislodging the roof. Subsequently, naval boats successfully completed the rescue.

The roads in Badulla's central district stayed impassable, isolating numerous villages and blocking the delivery of relief supplies. "Our village mourns the loss of two residents," stated Saman Kumara from Maspanna, a village in Badulla, one of the districts most severely affected. "We are trapped in the village, and entry is impossible for anyone as landslides have obstructed all access roads. There is no food, and our reserves of potable water are nearly depleted."

Authorities reported that approximately one-third of the nation faced a lack of electricity and running water due to the collapse of power lines and the flooding of water purification plants. Additionally, internet services experienced interruptions.

Cyclone Ditwah departed from the island on Saturday and made its way towards neighboring India. Chennai Airport in India grounded 54 flights as the cyclone neared, as the meteorological office predicts intense downpours and powerful gusts in the coming 48 hours.

The Sri Lankan authorities have called for global assistance and encouraged Sri Lankans living overseas to contribute financially to aid the impacted communities. India swiftly took the lead in responding, dispatching two aircraft filled with relief supplies and deploying a pair of transport helicopters accompanied by a rescue team consisting of 22 members. During a pre-scheduled goodwill visit to Colombo, an Indian warship contributed its provisions to aid the victims. Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi conveyed his sympathies regarding the fatalities in Sri Lanka and affirmed that New Delhi stood ready to dispatch additional assistance.

Heavy rainfall on Saturday exacerbated flooding in low-lying regions, particularly impacting those residing along the Kelani River's banks, which empties into the Indian Ocean from Colombo, leading officials to mandate evacuations. Although most of the country, including the capital, had seen a reduction in rainfall, the northern region of the island continued to experience showers.

Since 2017, the cyclone stands as Sri Lanka's most lethal natural calamity, when floods and landslides resulted in over 200 fatalities and caused the displacement of hundreds of thousands. In June 2003, the most severe flooding since the beginning of the century resulted in the deaths of 254 people.
