Emergency shelters have been established in Victoria and Saanich in response to harsh cold and powerful winds, triggering an Extreme Weather Response throughout the region.

Environment Canada has forecast strong winds increasing to 30 kilometres per hour, with gusts possibly reaching 70 kilometres per hour near the Juan de Fuca Strait, before easing overnight. Overnight temperatures are expected to fall to approximately 1 C, with even chillier conditions anticipated further inland.

In Saanich, an Extreme Weather Response shelter has been launched at Broadview United Church, located at 3703 St. Aidan's St. The operation is managed by Our Place Society, with financial backing from the District of Saanich provided by BC Housing. The shelter offers 20 overnight beds, featuring 25 provisional winter shelter spots and 25 fixed sleeping accommodations.

The spaces will be accessible as long as severe weather conditions satisfy provincial activation standards, which may encompass freezing temperatures, prolonged rain, or prolonged snowfall. The District explains that Extreme Weather Response beds are distinct from standard nightly shelter accommodations. These EWR spaces become operational solely upon the anticipation of dangerous weather conditions.

Residents of Saanich also have the option to utilize shelter services provided by the Victoria Native Friendship Centre, which accommodates 50 individuals.

Saanich has additionally made daytime public warming facilities available. During normal business hours, residents can seek warmth at municipal recreation centers and the Saanich municipal hall. The available facilities are Saanich Commonwealth Place, Gordon Head Recreation Centre, Cedar Hill Recreation and Arts Centre, and G.R. Pearkes Recreation Centre. The district acknowledges that operational hours might fluctuate in response to severe weather conditions because of staffing availability.

In Victoria, the city reports that its collaborators at the Salvation Army ARC have increased capacity by providing 30 additional shelter mats under the Emergency Weather Response initiative. Overnight accommodations are accessible at 525 Johnson St beginning at 8 p.m. on Friday and concluding at 7 a.m. on Saturday. The facility accommodates individuals of all genders and has a strict no-pet policy.
