# Climate Change Driving Australian Relocation Decisions, Study Finds

A comprehensive study conducted by Charles Sturt University reveals that climate change and extreme weather conditions are significantly influencing Australians' relocation decisions.

According to the survey, 41 percent of Australians—two out of five—regard Tasmania as the most secure state or territory to reside in amid climate change. Concerns about climate are increasingly prompting internal migration, as more Australians relocate from regions susceptible to climate threats to safer locales.

The survey, encompassing nearly 2,000 Australian participants, found that 22 percent of Australians planning to relocate within the next year attribute their decision to the impacts of climate change or severe weather conditions. For those who have experienced severe weather conditions, 36 percent indicate that their relocation is partially influenced by the repercussions of climate change.

Over the past six years, 14 percent of individuals who have changed residences report that climate change was a partial factor in their decision to move. For individuals residing in regional NSW, Brisbane, and regional Queensland, climate considerations weigh more heavily in relocation choices compared to other areas.

More than one-third of Australians express concerns about relocating due to apprehensions regarding severe weather conditions affecting their current residences. Those who are most concerned about climate change consider relocating more frequently.

The findings emerge from two reports authored by Clive Hamilton, AM, Professor of Public Ethics at Charles Sturt University, as part of the Living Hot Project—a seven-piece series derived from the inaugural nationwide Climate Adaptation Survey examining how Australians perceive and adapt to living in a warming world.

"As Australia's climate heats up and an increasing number of families experience extreme weather conditions, concerns about climate change will grow, prompting many Australians to consider moving to more secure regions of the country," stated Professor Hamilton.

Professor Hamilton remarked that authorities are overlooking the planning consequences as increasing numbers of Australians move from high-risk regions to areas deemed safer from climate impacts.

"The threat to social cohesion arises as affluent individuals have the means to relocate to climate-safe areas, leaving those with fewer resources to endure the challenges of floods, wildfires, and severe heat conditions," he noted.

When respondents were questioned about which state or territory will offer the greatest safety as climate change progresses, Tasmania emerged as the definitive front-runner, favored by 41 percent of Australians, leaving other states trailing considerably. Tasmania is particularly chosen by individuals deeply concerned about climate change and anticipating significantly warmer conditions by 2050. These individuals typically possess advanced educational qualifications and tend to support progressive political parties through their voting choices.

"When the migration from the mainland to Tasmania occurs, it will be spearheaded by individuals most concerned about climate change—highly educated and left-wing," said Professor Hamilton.

When asked which area or region within their preferred state will be safest, three out of five respondents select cities or towns rather than rural or remote areas. Young adults tend to favor residing in suburban and peripheral urban areas, whereas older Australians are more likely to favour country towns.

"Despite the common perception of self-reliant, isolated farms, a majority of Australians consider residing in established towns and cities to be the safest option as global temperatures rise," remarked Professor Hamilton. "Community linkages and collective resources are regarded as crucial safeguards against the pressures of living amidst a shifting climate."

The survey was developed and commissioned by Clive Hamilton, Professor of Public Ethics at Charles Sturt University, and executed by the esteemed public opinion firm Roy Morgan Research.
