Floods and landslides on Sumatra's western island have claimed 174 lives, with approximately 80 individuals remaining missing, according to a disaster official on Friday. The National Disaster Management Agency reported that torrential monsoon rains caused rivers in North Sumatra province to overflow on Tuesday, with water sweeping through mountainside villages and affecting more than 3,200 homes and structures.

Fatalities in North Sumatra have climbed to 116, with an additional 25 confirmed dead in Aceh. In West Sumatra, rescuers have discovered 23 bodies, according to National Disaster Mitigation Agency chief Suharyanto.

Thousands of homes in parts of Aceh and West Sumatra were inundated, with many submerged up to their roofs. The disaster mitigation agency in West Sumatra noted that over 17,000 homes were flooded, compelling around 23,000 people to seek refuge in temporary shelters. The floods and landslides not only destroyed rice fields, livestock, and public facilities but also severed bridges and roads, isolating residents.

The extreme weather was driven by tropical cyclone Senyar, which formed in the Strait of Malacca. Achadi Subarkah Raharjo from Indonesia's Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysical Agency explained that torrential rains triggered landslides that sent mud and rocks crashing onto hilly hamlets. Senyar heightened rainfall, fierce winds, and elevated waves in Aceh, North Sumatra, West Sumatra, Riau, and adjacent areas before fading.

"We have extended the extreme weather warning due to the robust water vapor supply and shifting atmospheric dynamics," Raharjo stated. He cautioned that the ongoing unstable atmospheric conditions could prolong the extreme weather while the cyclone system remains active. The persistent downpours have rendered the steep, waterlogged terrain extremely susceptible to disasters, he noted.

In Indonesia, an archipelago with 17,000 islands, seasonal rains often trigger flooding and landslides, impacting millions residing in mountainous regions or near fertile flood plains.
