COLOMBO, Dec. 9 (Xinhua) Emergency flood relief supplies sent by the Chinese government reached Colombo on Monday morning and were officially transferred to Sri Lankan authorities.

Chinese Ambassador to Sri Lanka Qi Zhenhong, Sri Lanka's Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation Anura Karunathilake, and Deputy Minister of Defense Aruna Jayasekera attended the handover ceremony.

Karunathilake expressed sincere gratitude for China's prompt and generous support, highlighting that the supplies delivered on Monday meet the critical needs of Sri Lanka. He emphasized that China has consistently offered assistance to Sri Lanka during challenging times throughout the years. Amid the ongoing crisis, substantial aid has been extended by the Chinese government, Chinese businesses, and the Chinese community residing in Sri Lanka, he noted. He expressed gratitude to the Chinese government and its citizens for their generous support and reiterated his aspiration for the lasting friendship between Sri Lanka and China.

Qi mentioned that in response to this serious natural disaster, all pertinent Chinese agencies promptly took action to offer assistance to their Sri Lankan counterparts. He observed that both Chinese enterprises and the Chinese diaspora in Sri Lanka have proactively engaged in aiding the local disaster relief initiatives. He noted that China's all-round support once again demonstrates that China is a trustworthy and reliable good friend and true friend of Sri Lanka. China remains committed to extending support to Sri Lanka to the best of its abilities, as it has consistently done in the past, and to jointly build a China-Sri Lanka community with a shared future.

Among the supplies sent were tents, bed sheets, life jackets, and blankets, forming a component of the Chinese government's humanitarian aid to Sri Lanka.

Sri Lanka's Disaster Management Center reported that Cyclone Ditwah has unleashed devastating weather conditions across the nation, resulting in the tragic loss of over 600 lives and impacting over 2 million residents. The cyclone demolished over 4,000 homes and inflicted partial damage on upwards of 60,000 residences across the country.
