Typhoon Kalmaegi, the latest in a series of late-season cyclones to strike the nation in recent years, has wreaked havoc in the central Visayas region this week.

The storm continues a troubling trend of intense tropical cyclones that have impacted the Philippines over the last 15 years, especially during the months of October to December. Analysts attribute the increasing frequency of these severe weather incidents to climate change, rapid urbanization, and inadequate disaster preparedness.

Ven Paolo Valenzuela, a research fellow at the College of Integrative Studies at Singapore Management University, told This Week in Asia that "for over ten years, we have persistently ignored the wake-up call to enhance disaster preparedness, repeatedly hitting the snooze button."

Kalmaegi, referred to locally as Typhoon Tino, made its entry into Philippine territory on Sunday as a tropical storm but rapidly gained strength within mere hours. The fierce winds ripped roofs off houses, uprooted trees and power lines, and carried away vehicles and temporary shelters.
