COLOMBO - Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday, seeking global aid as the death toll from floods and landslides induced by Cyclone Ditwah climbed to 153, with 191 individuals unaccounted for.

The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reported that the severe weather conditions have devastated more than 20,000 homes, forcing 108,000 individuals into government-run temporary shelters. DMC spokesman Pradeep Kodippili stated that 798,000 people needed aid after being temporarily displaced by the floods.

Personnel from the army, navy, and air force, along with civilian workers and volunteers, have been mobilized to support the relief operations.

A passenger, recounting from the hospital, described how navy sailors assisted them in reaching the roof of a nearby house, using ropes to navigate through the rising floodwaters. A helicopter's initial rescue attempt was thwarted when the rotor's downdraft risked dislodging the roof they were on, but naval boats subsequently completed the rescue.

In the central district of Badulla, roads remained impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. "We lost two people in our village, others have taken refuge in a temple and one house still standing," reported Saman Kumara from Maspanna, one of the hardest-hit areas in Badulla. "We can't leave the village, and no one can enter as all routes are obstructed by landslides. We lack food and are running out of clean water," he conveyed to the News Centre via telephone.

About a third of the country was without electricity or running water because power lines had collapsed and water purification facilities were inundated. Internet connections were also disrupted.

Cyclone Ditwah moved away from the island on Saturday and was heading towards neighbouring India. Chennai Airport in India cancelled 54 flights in response to the cyclone's approach, with meteorologists predicting extremely heavy rainfall and strong winds for the next 48 hours.

The Sri Lankan government has issued an appeal for international aid and urged Sri Lankans abroad to make cash donations to support affected communities. An Indian warship, which was already in Colombo on a previously planned goodwill visit, donated its rations to assist the victims.
