Unrelenting extreme weather has battered North Sumatra for several consecutive days, inundating sections of the Tapanuli Selatan district beginning on Monday, as reported by the national disaster agency, BNPB.

Abdul Muhari, spokesperson for BNPB, expressed in a statement that in Tapanuli Selatan, the calamity of floods and landslides has led to the tragic loss of eight lives, injuries to 58 individuals, and the displacement of 2,851 residents who were forced to evacuate. Approximately 13 individuals remain unaccounted for.

He stated that the local disaster agency has dispatched heavy machinery to remove debris that has obstructed road access to the district. He mentioned that flooding and landslides have impacted at least three additional districts within the province.

As reported by the Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency, the extreme weather conditions are attributed to cyclone seeds, which are weather formations with the capacity to develop into tropical cyclones.

The yearly monsoon season, usually occurring from June to September, frequently results in intense rainfall, which can lead to landslides, sudden floods, and the spread of waterborne illnesses. Alterations in climate have affected storm patterns, modifying the length and severity of the season, which results in increased rainfall, more frequent flash floods, and intensified wind gusts.

This month, landslides caused by intense rainfall in Central Java claimed the lives of at least 38 individuals.
