# Severe Storms in Sumatra Leave Over 400 Dead, 290,000 Displaced

JAKARTA — The death toll from severe storms that have battered three provinces on Sumatra has surged past 400, with an equivalent number of individuals unaccounted for, as the repercussions of intense weather across Southeast Asia this past week persistently escalate.

Southern Thailand, northern Malaysia, and Vietnam have similarly suffered severe impacts from the floods, which are largely being linked to climate change. The total death toll across the region exceeds 700.

Suhariyanto, the head of Indonesia's National Disaster Mitigation Agency (BNPB), said on Saturday evening that North Sumatra bears the brunt, experiencing torrential downpours since November 25 as a result of Cyclone Senyar and Cyclone Koto, which are uncommon meteorological events in the Sumatra area. On Sunday evening, the BNPB reported that the confirmed death toll in the impacted region had risen to 442, with 402 individuals still unaccounted for.

He further mentioned that West Sumatra and Aceh were among the provinces that faced substantial rainfall. Approximately 1.1 million individuals have experienced the impact, with 290,000 compelled to leave their residences.

Landslides obstructing access routes in highland regions have impeded both evacuations and the delivery of aid. "Certain areas remain inaccessible," Suhariyanto stated, noting that extended power outages and disrupted communications were additionally hindering both rescuers and aid teams. "In response, we have supplied electric generators and Starlink units to local authorities, evacuation facilities, and the armed forces and law enforcement," he stated.

The evacuation efforts across the three provinces are spearheaded by BNPB, alongside the National Search and Rescue Agency, the military, and the police. "The unstable weather and soil conditions pose significant difficulties," stated Hery Marantika, who leads the Search and Rescue Office in Medan, North Sumatra.

The Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency (BMKG) issued a warning last Wednesday regarding the possible effects of tropical cyclones Senyar and Koto on three provinces, including Riau and the Riau Islands. "Tropical cyclones have seen an increase in frequency over the last decade, typically occurring from November until March or April of the subsequent year," stated Dwikorita Karnawati, Chairwoman of BMKG. She attributed the rising occurrence of cyclones to climate change.

Rianda Purba, who leads the Indonesian Forum for the Environment (Walhi) in North Sumatra, pointed out that Central Tapanuli, the most severely affected regency in the province, is situated along the slopes of the Bukit Barisan mountain ridge on Sumatra. "Land cover has markedly diminished in the upstream and riverbank zones of this area, primarily as a result of deforestation linked to the Batang Toru hydropower project, as well as local logging operations and gold mining activities," he told Nikkei Asia.

Although the storms have impacted three provinces, collectively housing over 20 million residents, the central government has refrained from announcing a national disaster, leaving the determination of emergency status to the discretion of individual provincial or regency governments. "Indonesia has exclusively recognized the COVID-19 pandemic and the 2004 tsunami as national disasters," stated Suhariyanto.

Analysts are also warning about the availability of mitigation funding, with the budgets of the three key disaster management agencies—BNPB, BMKG, and the National Search and Rescue Agency—being reduced earlier this year to reallocate funds towards President Prabowo Subianto's major initiatives. Cuts to BMKG are a primary concern for observers, as they impair the nation's ability to monitor and issue early warnings effectively.

On Friday, Prabowo stated that the government responded promptly, delivering assistance both by land and through aerial means. "The conditions are very difficult, with many roads blocked, and the adverse weather conditions continue to prevent our helicopters and planes from landing," he said.

The central government has deployed three Hercules C-130 aircraft alongside an A-400 aircraft, all loaded with emergency provisions for the residents impacted by the disaster.
