# Record-Breaking Lightning Bolt Stretched 515 Miles Across Central United States

A lightning flash extending 515 miles from Texas to Missouri has been recognized as the longest electrical bolt in the world, according to the World Meteorological Organization.

An analysis of satellite imagery from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's GOES-16 satellite uncovered that a lightning strike on October 22, 2017, surpassed the length of a previous record-holder by approximately 38 miles. The previous longest lightning strike was thought to have occurred on April 29, 2020, spanning across Texas, Louisiana, and Mississippi and measuring 477 miles long.

NOAA announced in an August 5 press release that the lightning bolt from 2017 had indeed set the new record. The World Meteorological Organization set this new benchmark, noting that each of the lightning occurrences were identified as "megaflashes"—extremely long duration and distance lightning discharge events.

The WMO reported that this unprecedented lightning bolt originated within a significant thunderstorm system in a recognized "storm hotspot" located in the Great Plains area of the United States. According to the WMO announcement, "The stretch from eastern Texas to near Kansas City mirrors the span from Paris to Venice in Europe. A car journey would last around eight to nine hours, while a commercial flight would require at least 90 minutes to traverse this distance."

Professor Randall Cerveny, featured in Weather and Climate Extremes for the WMO, noted that recent progress in science and technology now enables us to evaluate extreme weather conditions, including the measurement of lightning distances. "This new record clearly demonstrates the incredible power of the natural environment," Cerveny remarked. "It is likely that even greater extremes still exist, and that we will be able to observe them as additional high-quality lightning measurements accumulate over time."

The NOAA GOES-16 satellite, part of the Geostationary Operational Environmental Satellite program, captured images of both megaflashes in 2017 and 2020. Launched in 2016, GOES-16 marked the beginning of the GOES-R satellite series, which comprises four satellites in total. The series was completed with the launch of its last satellite in June 2024. GOES-16 remains positioned over specific points on the Earth's surface in geostationary orbit.

## Extreme Weather Impacts

According to NOAA, the United States experiences approximately 25 million cloud-to-ground lightning strikes each year. The U.S. government, via NOAA and NASA, conducts ongoing monitoring and research on lightning activity to enhance weather prediction and comprehension. Lightning is associated with 24,000 fatalities and 240,000 injuries globally, along with countless wildfires and financial losses amounting to millions in property damage annually.

The National Centers for Environmental Information within NOAA monitors the financial impact of extreme weather events, which amounts to billions of dollars. In 2024, they documented 27 significant natural disasters, comprising 17 severe storms such as tornado outbreaks, high winds, and hailstorms, alongside 5 tropical cyclones and 2 winter storm/cold waves. According to NCEI's website, these 27 events collectively incurred costs totaling $182.7 billion. This follows 2023, in which there were a record 28 separate billion-dollar events.

Wildfires, droughts, and heatwaves are also part of these costly billion-dollar occurrences. Meteorological forecasts predict that intensified weather phenomena, along with rising temperatures and shifting climate patterns, will lead to stronger storms. In January, NCEI announced that the financial impact of natural disasters in the U.S. could increase by several billion dollars compared to 2024.
