# Mongolia's Dzud: A Devastating Climate Phenomenon

Locally referred to as the 'white death,' a dzud is a severe climatic phenomenon in Mongolia, occurring when a summer drought precedes a brutal winter. Temperatures can plunge to below -30 degrees, with heavy snowfall enveloping the landscape. Although these events traditionally happened every few years, climate change has altered their frequency, leading to some areas experiencing them annually.

## The Impact of Dzud

The winter of 2022-2023 was exceptionally harsh. Mongolia experienced 90% of its land enveloped in thick snow cover, marking it as the heaviest snowfall in 49 years. Millions of livestock perished not only from the severe cold but also from malnutrition, as droughts over the summer months had severely limited food cultivation. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), 5.9 million animals died in 2023 due to the dzud, and 9% of the country's entire herd was lost.

Tragically, the nation's herding communities bear the brunt of this impact. The loss of livestock often equates to a loss of livelihood, as many families rely on selling their produce to sustain themselves. As the snow eventually melts, the resulting water triggers widespread flooding throughout the country. These floods further devastate infrastructure and grazing areas, while heightening the risk of food and waterborne illnesses, especially in impoverished regions with rudimentary sanitation systems. Thus, the dzud manifests as both a natural calamity and a socio-economic crisis.

## Ongoing Intervention Efforts

Many aid organizations and government officials have been assisting the country. The WHO and World Bank have collaborated with the Mongolian Ministry of Health to ensure those in rural areas receive the help they need. Frontline health workers with medical supplies and the capacity to offer psychological support to those affected have been offering aid in the 21 affected provinces.

The World Bank has also been working alongside the meteorological and environmental monitoring agency to compile a comprehensive disaster risk financing strategy. Weather data gathered is used to trigger the release of funding ahead of catastrophic weather events, ensuring herding families are protected with financial assistance before the dzud even begins.

One year after the 2024 dzud, the Mongolian Red Cross Society has been particularly helpful. It has continued to help herder households get back on their feet, delivering critical humanitarian assistance where needed and offering services such as psychological support to the families who suffered the hardest hit.

## Looking Ahead

Yet the efforts of organizations like the WHO, World Bank, and Mongolian Red Cross Society show that early intervention, social protection programs, and community support can make a difference.
