Research commissioned by the Energy & Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) reveals that over the last five years, extreme weather conditions have significantly impacted the mental health of farmers.

Compiled by Grounded Research and featuring input from 300 farmers, the report indicates that nearly every British farmer (98%) has encountered severe weather conditions over the last five years, where extreme rainfall (86%), drought (78%), and heatwaves (54%) are the most frequently encountered phenomena.

Consequently, there has been a significant decline in mental health, with 92% of farmers experiencing anxiety and 34% feeling "very anxious." Almost 60% reported experiencing feelings of depression, and 6% reported feeling "very depressed."

According to the authors, these sentiments stemmed from concerns over the potential loss of crops and livestock (78%), the expenses involved in recuperating from severe weather events (68%), and which crops to cultivate due to the erratic nature of the weather (52%).

In total, 43% of farmers expressed concern that they might not earn sufficient income to sustain their farming operations during times of severe weather conditions.

The level of concern among farmers was evident, with approximately half (51%) expressing weekly anxiety over extreme weather, and nearly one in five (19%) experiencing this worry almost daily.

Joanne Coates, a Yorkshire Dales beef farmer, noted that erratic weather conditions have increased hesitance among farmers to leave their properties, fearing they might need to swiftly address an unforeseen flood or other sudden emergencies. "This prevents individuals from engaging in community areas, thereby intensifying feelings of isolation and loneliness," Ms Coates remarked.

Colin Chappell, a crop farmer from North Lincolnshire, remarked that weather conditions have the power to determine the success or failure of a farm. "This constant uncertainty takes a real toll on mental health," he remarked.

In spite of these significant impacts on their wellbeing, many farmers are not seeking help.

A spokesperson for the Royal Agricultural Benevolent Institution (Rabi) noted that their Big Farming Survey had earlier revealed that extreme and unpredictable weather ranks among the primary contributors to stress and poor mental health within the farming community. "We urge farmers facing challenges with their mental health or wellbeing to reach out to Rabi's toll-free helpline at 0800 188 4444," the spokesperson added.
