Super Typhoon Ragasa, recognized as the strongest tropical cyclone of the year, struck on Wednesday, September 24, resulting in fatalities and injuries and causing extensive devastation across southern China, the Philippines, Taiwan, and Hong Kong during its course.

Originating in the western Pacific, this super typhoon intensified as sea temperatures rose, eventually escalating to a category 5 storm—the pinnacle classification on the Saffir-Simpson hurricane wind scale—exhibiting wind velocities of 260 km/h.

Is it still appropriate to label extreme weather phenomena, such as super typhoon Ragasa, merely as "natural events" when scientific evidence unmistakably indicates that human-induced climate warming has been and continues to intensify these occurrences? Ample evidence demonstrates that our reliance on fossil fuels is directly linked to the escalation of extreme weather phenomena.

The limited photographs of super typhoon Ragasa fail to entirely encompass the extent of the destruction, yet they serve as evidence that nature's fury possesses the complete capacity to obliterate so much that is dear to us, unless we unite to implement systemic reforms and prioritize human welfare over financial gain.
