A Senate inquiry has been backed by Labor, the coalition and the Greens into the catastrophe that has claimed the lives of tens of thousands of marine creatures since its detection near the Fleurieu Peninsula in March.

The spread along the coastline has triggered a collaborative $28 million investment package, co-financed by the South Australian and federal governments, aimed at clean-up, recovery, research, and industry backing.

Following the approval of the Senate inquiry on Wednesday night, Nationals senator Ross Cadell remarked that the widespread backing indicated the matter transcended political boundaries.

"Whether it be flood, drought, bushfire, or algal bloom, our communities need support, not politics," Cadell said.

"This investigation offers an opportunity to fully understand the tangible consequences of this ecological catastrophe and evaluate the adequacy of the existing disaster relief funding framework."

"Fish, dolphins, rays and sharks washing up on the beach dead, murky groundwater and a dense layer of foam on beaches that are typically stunning," she said.

Its conclusion remains uncertain, both in terms of timing and manner.

"The algal bloom was unprecedented, leaving us uncertain about its duration and the additional consequences it may bring, but it underscores the urgency for climate action," she remarked.

On Tuesday, Premier Peter Malinauskas declared that the SA government would provide a matching $14 million commitment.

The package, crafted by federal Environment Minister Murray Watt, allocates $8.5 million toward a coastal science and research network designed to enhance the early detection and monitoring of harmful algal bloom species.

A rapid assessment costing $3 million will evaluate the effects of the bloom on fish stocks and fisheries, while a newly established $2 million national testing lab in SA will eliminate the necessity of sending samples to New Zealand for analysis.
