As extreme weather events become more frequent, substantial yearly economic losses are emerging as Hong Kong's "new normal," experts warned on Thursday.

Typhoon Ragasa prompted Hong Kong's top-tier No. 10 typhoon alert, halting city activities for almost two days. Colossal waves and storm surges wreaked havoc on coastal restaurants, with owners reporting millions of dollars in damages.

"Economic losses due to extreme weather are set to become the standard for Hong Kong and globally," stated Gary Ng Cheuk-yan, a senior economist at Natixis Corporate and Investment Bank.

Economist Simon Lee Siu-po quantified the projected financial impact of Ragasa to fall within the range of HK$2 billion to HK$3 billion, equivalent to US$257 million to US$386 million.
