# Wildfire Destroys Homes in Historic California Gold Rush Town

TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif. (AP) — A rapidly advancing wildfire destroyed homes in a historic California Gold Rush town established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp.

The blaze swiftly grew to encompass 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of Chinese Camp and nearby roads on Tuesday, as reported by CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency.

This blaze is part of the TCU September Lightning Complex, which has scorched over 19 square miles across various areas in Calaveras and Tuolumne counties. By Wednesday, the fire had expanded unchecked with no containment.

In the rural town, at least five homes were engulfed in flames, featuring a combination of standalone houses and mobile residences. A recreational vehicle situated on the premises sustained damage due to the blaze.

On Tuesday evening, outside a residence, seven individuals swiftly cleared large tree branches from the building and tossed sand onto the flames, frantically working to prevent the fire from spreading from the adjacent house. For approximately half an hour, they continued their efforts until firefighters showed up.

Fire authorities have sought extra assets, including fire trucks, bulldozers, and a plane.

Dubbed the 6-5 Fire, this blaze was ignited by lightning, according to CalFire. It stands among over a dozen fires that sprang up across California on Tuesday.

No instant accounts of casualties or fatalities were available.

During the Gold Rush, thousands of Chinese immigrants arrived in California, where they encountered significant persecution, including the imposition of an excessive Foreign Miners Tax aimed at deterring them from engaging in mining activities.

Chinese Camp was established by Chinese miners who relocated after being expelled from a nearby settlement. Initially named Camp Washington, the area underwent a name change to honor the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers.
