Flash floods in China's Gansu province have resulted in at least 10 fatalities, with 33 individuals reported missing, according to state media.

Beginning on August 7, unrelenting downpours have led to sudden flooding. State broadcaster CCTV announced on Friday at 3:30pm (07:30 GMT) on August 8 that 10 individuals had died, with 33 still unaccounted for.

Chinese President Xi Jinping has commanded rescue efforts to be carried out with complete dedication and maximum exertion to locate the missing individuals, CCTV reported. Faced with the "frequent occurrence of extreme weather," Xi instructed all regions to "resolutely overcome complacency" and strengthen efforts to identify risks.

Video released by Chinese fire officials on Weibo, a social media platform, depicted rescuers leading villagers through rapidly flowing grey water. Images released by the Gansu government depicted roads blanketed with silt and sizable stones.

In recent weeks, China's northern and southern regions have been battered by record-breaking rainfall, which meteorologists characterize as extreme weather associated with climate change. Since late July, severe rainfall and subsequent flooding have resulted in the deaths of at least 60 individuals throughout northern China, including the capital city of Beijing.

Previous flooding disasters in China have claimed numerous lives, with incidents including 31 individuals trapped at an elderly care facility among the casualties, and severe rainfall in Beijing resulting in the tragic loss of 30 lives. Heavy rainfall has prompted flood warnings across 11 provinces, with deadly rains and landslides forcing mass evacuations in northern China.
