The Director of IMD, Mrutyunjay Mohapatra, indicated that although light rain will persist throughout the coming week, there is no forecast for heavy, widespread rainfall in the short term.

Anticipated Weather Pattern Emerging Near September 20

Mohapatra highlighted the potential emergence of a new low-pressure system in the Bay of Bengal approximately on September 20. "There is a chance of wind gusts developing, though a cyclone is improbable for now. The forecast will become clearer in the coming days," he remarked, noting that the trajectory and strength of the system are contingent upon evolving meteorological conditions.

No Severe Weather Threat

He assured that there is no imminent threat of cyclones or severe weather conditions to Odisha. The earlier low-pressure system, which caused heavy rainfall in the coastal and western districts, has now moved towards north Telangana and Vidarbha, resulting in a steady decrease in rainfall throughout the state.

Localized Rainfall Expected to Persist

Although the overall rainfall is diminishing, the IMD has cautioned about the possibility of isolated heavy showers and thunderstorms, especially in areas susceptible to flooding and low-lying zones. Officials will persist in observing the Bay of Bengal for any major changes that may affect both coastal and interior regions.
