# Extreme Wildfires in Spain and Portugal Made 40 Times More Likely by Climate Change

Preliminary analysis indicates that the extreme weather conditions which triggered the devastating wildfires in Spain and Portugal last month were made 40 times more probable due to climate change, according to researchers from the World Weather Attribution network.

The lethal wildfires ravaged 500,000 hectares (1.2 million acres) across the Iberian peninsula in just weeks and were additionally 30% more intense than scientists would have anticipated in a climate change-free world.

"The magnitude of these fires has been truly remarkable," noted Clair Barnes, a climate scientist at Imperial College London and one of the study's co-authors. "Climate change is exacerbating hotter, drier, and increasingly flammable conditions, leading to fires that burn with unmatched ferocity."

The researchers determined that these conditions might have arisen once every 500 years in the preindustrial climate, whereas now they are anticipated to occur every 15 years in the current global climate, heated by pollution that traps heat due to coal, oil, and gas combustion.

According to the researchers, the influence of climate disruption on extreme heat was even more pronounced. Prior to industrialisation, the 10-day maximum temperatures observed in the region last month were projected to occur once every 2,500 years. Now, this phenomenon is anticipated to happen every 13 years.

The research, pending submission for peer review, depended on direct weather observations instead of the typical climate model analyses the group performs shortly after severe weather events. A comprehensive study they released last week on the Turkish and Greek wildfires revealed that climate disruption had increased the likelihood of such extreme weather conditions by a factor of 10.

Modifications in land utilization have heightened the escalating regional threat of uncontrolled wildfires. Numerous warm Mediterranean nations have grappled with the consequences of rural exodus and demographic ageing. As rural youth migrate to urban areas, they abandon farmland, resulting in neglected fields with dense vegetation that is highly flammable.

David Garcia, an applied mathematician at the University of Alicante, noted that discussions in Spain have centered on diminishing rural activities and the consequent expansion of vegetation. "Nonetheless, the significant impact of climate change on these fires remains largely under-discussed, despite clear evidence of its substantial influence," he added.

On Monday, Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez unveiled specifics of a comprehensive 10-point climate initiative designed to address the growing severity of extreme weather conditions. During a discussion with the Guardian, the centre-left leader cautioned that mainstream rightwing parties were jeopardizing climate initiatives by embracing far-right rhetoric.

"The issue confronting us today is that conventional right-wing parties might not refute the scientific truth, yet they conduct themselves as though climate change is not a reality," Sánchez commented. "And this is the biggest problem."
