# Canyon Fire Forces Evacuation of Nearly 17,000 Residents in Southern California

Nearly 17,000 residents across Ventura and Los Angeles Counties were instructed to evacuate as the rapidly advancing Canyon Fire posed a significant threat.

The Canyon Fire sparked into existence at approximately 1:25 p.m. on Thursday, August 8, as reported by the California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection, commonly known as Cal Fire. According to reports, the fire initially spanned 30 acres near the Lake Piru reservoir in Ventura County.

By Thursday night, the wildfire had exploded in size to cover 4,856 acres, challenging firefighters attempting to control it.

At 10:48 a.m. local time, Cal Fire issued an update alerting those in the vicinity that the Canyon Fire was advancing swiftly, spreading at a rapid rate and endangering the 60kv powerlines and posing a threat to the communities of Hasley Canyon and Hathaway Ranch.

"Immediate threat to life. This is a lawful order to LEAVE NOW. The area is lawfully closed to public access," urged Cal Fire's incident report.

"Residents of Santa Clarita, Hasley Canyon, and Val Verde, heed evacuation directives with utmost seriousness — when first responders say GO, leave immediately. Keep aware — please don't risk lives," tweeted Kathryn Barger, Los Angeles County Supervisor.

A general update dated August 8 from the National Interagency Coordination Center revealed that, so far in 2025, 42,670 fires have occurred, surpassing the ten-year average of 35,620 fires.

A series of out-of-season wildfires devastated swaths of Los Angeles in January. In July, a brushfire ravaged the Grand Canyon's North Rim, leading to the destruction of historic structures. By August, the Gifford Fire had rapidly ascended to become California's second-largest wildfire of the year. By August 8, it remained uncontained.

Although wildfires are not technically classified as weather, they are considered part of extreme weather phenomena. Fires, deluges, tropical storms, and intense heatwaves have consistently featured within typical weather cycles, yet extreme weather persists unabated, with severe weather events becoming increasingly frequent, more intense, and hazardous.

In July, a report detailing the severe effects of extreme weather on the continent was released by the European Commission. The Commission highlighted that hot extremes, which previously occurred once every ten years, are now experienced almost three times as frequently and are 1.2°C (approximately 2.2 degrees Fahrenheit) hotter in temperature, as reported by the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change.

With the increasing frequency of extreme weather events globally, recognizing pivotal climate concerns serves as an essential safeguard.

Evacuation centers were established by the Red Cross to assist residents from Ventura County and Los Angeles County. On Friday, containment of the Canyon Fire had reached 25%.
