PESHAWAR, Pakistan (AP) — Heavy monsoon rains triggered flash floods in northern Pakistan, sweeping away multiple tourist vehicles and resulting in the deaths of at least three people, with another 15 unaccounted for, officials said Tuesday.

According to Pakistani rescue officials, the unusually heavy monsoon rains beginning June 26 have resulted in the deaths of at least 225 individuals and left over 500 others injured nationwide. Experts attribute the uptick in extreme weather events in the area to the impacts of climate change.

A torrential downpour on Monday triggered flooding and landslides, leaving over 200 local tourists stranded and blocking a major highway, said Faizullah Faraq, a government spokesperson for the Gilgit-Baltistan region. Army helicopters facilitated the evacuation process.

Several vehicles were engulfed by landslide debris, and three bodies have been retrieved. Numerous vehicles lay entombed beneath the debris, prompting rescuers to deploy heavy equipment in their search for the missing tourists and locals.

Days prior, the National Disaster Management Authority released an updated flood alert, advising against travel to northern regions due to the risk of landslides and road obstructions.

This month, officials cautioned that another bout of severe weather, akin to the 2022 floods that inundated one-third of the nation and resulted in 1,737 fatalities, cannot be dismissed.
