TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif. (AP) — A rapidly advancing wildfire consumed homes in a California Gold Rush town on Wednesday, prompting evacuations as the blaze continued to expand unchecked.

The fire swiftly grew to encompass 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of the town of Chinese Camp and nearby highways on Tuesday, as reported by CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency. There were no immediate reports of injuries or fatalities.

This fire is among several blazes known as the TCU September Lightning Complex, which has consumed over 19 square miles across Calaveras and Tuolumne counties without any containment by Wednesday morning.

At least five residences were ablaze in the rural area, characterized by a combination of standalone and mobile homes.

On Tuesday evening, a group of seven individuals hastily relocated large tree branches away from a building and shoveled sand onto the flames in a desperate attempt to prevent the fire from spreading from the neighboring house. Their efforts lasted approximately 30 minutes until firefighters arrived. An RV on the property was damaged by the fire.

Fire officials have called for more resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft.

CalFire has determined that the fire originated from a lightning strike and is among over a dozen fires that ignited across California on Tuesday.

Chinese Camp was established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. During the Gold Rush, a multitude of Chinese immigrants arrived in California but were met with widespread discrimination and hostility, including the imposition of a steep Foreign Miners Tax intended to force them out of the mining industry. Initially named Camp Washington, the settlement was later renamed to honor the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers, as noted by Visit Tuolumne County.
