Since 2020, the UK has lost an entire year's supply of bread as extreme weather conditions have devastated crop harvests, according to a report from the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU).

Climate breakdown has intensified droughts and floods, resulting in a shortfall of more than 7 million tonnes in wheat production. Specialists at the ECIU have assessed that this quantity of wheat is sufficient to produce over 4 billion loaves of bread, equating to an entire year's provision.

This decade has seen two of the three poorest harvests ever recorded. The worst year was 2020 and the third worst was 2024. Extreme rainfall led to some of the poorest harvests on record, as farmers struggled to drill and manage crops. Conversely, this year has presented a different struggle, with crops enduring the harsh conditions of one of the hottest and driest springs and summers documented. Faced with unprecedented heat, drought conditions, and restrictions on water use, farmers found it challenging to sow their crops.

With the drought conditions persisting this year, 2025 is anticipated to be another challenging year for crop yields. The situation is expected to deteriorate further as climate change leads to extended dry spells combined with more intense and severe rainfall, potentially resulting in floods.

Tom Lancaster, the analyst specializing in land, food, and farming for the ECIU, commented: "This exemplifies the reality of farming amidst climate change. Unpredictable weather patterns are rendering our bread less British, compelling millers to rely on imports because of deficiencies in UK yields, costing British farmers billions in lost income and reducing our self-sufficiency in our main staple crop."

The UK's wheat self-sufficiency decreased from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024. To produce flour for bread, cakes, and biscuits, millers are bringing in unprecedented quantities of wheat. Last autumn, wheat imports surged to twice the five-year average.

The scarcity of wheat is straining international supplies, while other nations face their own challenges as conflicts and severe weather conditions are reducing their crop yields.

Colin Chappell, a crop farmer from Lincolnshire, expressed: "Farming has never been more difficult. As a farmer, I'm accustomed to handling the harshest weather conditions, but the recent years have turned unmanageable. Extended periods of rain are succeeded by prolonged droughts, seemingly without respite, and I am now confronting a second consecutive disastrous harvest season in a row."

"I require additional assistance to adjust to these extreme conditions and fortify my business's resilience. However, with the green farming schemes in England currently closed, that support isn't available to me. Getting them open has to be a priority if farmers are to be able to continue to grow wheat and other key crops for the Great British public."
