Flash floods in Gansu province, located in northwestern China, have resulted in at least 10 fatalities and 33 individuals reported missing, according to state media.

Starting on August 7, relentless heavy rainfall led to sudden flash floods. By 3:30pm (07:30 GMT) on August 8, the death toll had reached 10, with 33 individuals unaccounted for, reported state broadcaster CCTV on Friday.

Chinese President Xi Jinping has called for "all-out" rescue efforts, demanding the "utmost effort" to locate and save those who are missing, according to CCTV.

In response to the "frequent occurrence of extreme weather," Xi ordered all regions to "resolutely overcome complacency" and strengthen efforts to identify risks.

Video footage posted by Chinese fire authorities on the social media site Weibo depicted rescuers leading villagers through torrents of grey floodwater in a village.

Images released by the Gansu government depicted roads blanketed with silt and sizable rocks.

In recent weeks, China has experienced heavy rainfall in both its northern and southern regions, which meteorologists characterize as extreme weather events associated with climate change.

Since late July, torrential rains and subsequent flooding have claimed the lives of at least 60 individuals in northern China, encompassing regions such as Beijing.
